==== In journals ====
* {{anchor|Elsevier}}Students at Iran's [[Sharif University of Technology]] published a paper in [[Elsevier]]'s ''Journal of Applied Mathematics and Computation''.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://ce.sharif.edu/~ghodsi/soft-group/misc/AMC-paper.pdf | title = Cooperative, Compact Algorithms for Randomized Algorithms | author = Rohollah Mosallahnezhad | url-status = dead | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20091229022456/http://ce.sharif.edu/~ghodsi/soft-group/misc/AMC-paper.pdf | archive-date = 2009-12-29 }}</ref>  The students wrote under the surname "MosallahNejad", which translates literally from [[Persian language]] (in spite of not being a traditional [[Persian name]]) as "from an Armed Breed".  The paper was subsequently removed when the publishers were informed that it was a joke paper.<ref>{{citation | doi = 10.1016/j.amc.2007.03.011 | title = REMOVED: Cooperative, compact algorithms for randomized algorithms | journal = Applied Mathematics and Computation | author = Rohollah Mosallahnezhad | year = 2007 }}</ref>
* {{anchor|Russia}}[[Mikhail Gelfand]] published a translation of the "Rooter" article in the Russian-language ''Journal of Scientific Publications of Aspirants and Doctorants'' in August 2008. Gelfand was protesting against the journal, which was apparently not peer-reviewed and was being used by Russian PhD candidates to publish in an "[[Academic ranks in Russia|accredited]]" scientific journal, charging them 4,000 Rubles to do so. The accreditation was revoked two weeks later.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.sciencepresse.qc.ca/actualite/2009/09/08/ordinateur-ecrit-mieux-tien|title=Mon ordinateur écrit mieux que le tien!|date=8 September 2009|work=Agence Science-Presse|language=fr|access-date=4 October 2011|location=Canada}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://pdos.csail.mit.edu/scigen/blog/index.php?entry=entry090108-122533|title=Rooter invades Russia|date=8 January 2009|work=SCIgen|access-date=4 October 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140403061348/https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/scigen/blog/index.php?entry=entry090108-122533|archive-date=2014-04-03|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.ntv.ru/novosti/141780/|script-title=ru:Группа отечественных ученых поставила эксперимент — смешала сложные термины случайным образом, а полученный текст отослала в один из научных журналов|last=Malozemov|first=Sergei|date=7 October 2008|work=NTV|language=ru|access-date=4 October 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Feedback|date=15 August 2009|work=New Scientist}}</ref> (See [[Dissernet]] for related information.)
*[[Springer Science+Business Media#History|Springer Science+Business Media]] and IEEE were also the subject of similar pranks.

=== Spoofing Google Scholar and ''h''-index calculators ===

Refereeing performed on behalf of the [[Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers]] has also been subject to criticism after fake papers were discovered in conference publications, most notably by Labbé and a researcher using the pseudonym of [[#Schlangemann|Schlangemann]].<ref>{{cite journal | first1 = Cyril | last1= Labbé | first2 = Dominique | last2 = Labbé |date= 2013 |title= Duplicate and fake publications in the scientific literature: how many SCIgen papers in computer science?|journal= Scientometrics |volume= 94|issue= 1|pages= 379–396 | doi=10.1007/s11192-012-0781-y| s2cid= 6889400 | url= https://hal.archives-ouvertes.fr/hal-00641906 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://retractionwatch.com/2014/02/24/springer-ieee-withdrawing-more-than-120-nonsense-papers/ |title=Springer, IEEE withdrawing more than 120 nonsense papers |last=Oransky |first=Ivan |date=February 24, 2014 |website=retractionwatch.com |publisher=WordPress.com |access-date=April 29, 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal | first = Paul Colin | last= de Gloucester |date= 2013 |title= Referees Often Miss Obvious Errors in Computer and Electronic Publications|url= http://www.tandfonline.com/eprint/bjYcIWGse8G2J3TVmMBD/full |journal= Accountability in Research: Policies and Quality Assurance |volume= 20|issue= 3|pages= 143–166 |doi=10.1080/08989621.2013.788379| pmid= 23672521 | bibcode= 2013ARPQ...20..143D | s2cid= 42975675 |url-access= subscription }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://beta.slashdot.org/story/111963 |title=Software-Generated Paper Accepted At IEEE Conference |last=Dawson |first=K. |date=December 23, 2008 |website=slashdot.org |publisher=Dice |access-date=April 29, 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://linux.slashdot.jp/story/08/12/24/0750222/IEEE%E3%82%AB%E3%83%B3%E3%83%95%E3%82%A1%E3%83%AC%E3%83%B3%E3%82%B9%E3%80%81%E8%87%AA%E5%8B%95%E7%94%9F%E6%88%90%E3%81%AE%E3%83%8B%E3%82%BB%E8%AB%96%E6%96%87%E3%82%92%E3%82%A2%E3%82%AF%E3%82%BB%E3%83%97%E3%83%88 |title=IEEEカンファレンス、自動生成のニセ論文をアクセプト |last=Hatta |first=Masayuki |date=December 24, 2008 |website=slashdot.jp |publisher=OSDN Corporation |language=Japanese |access-date=April 29, 2014 |archive-date=April 19, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140419094313/http://linux.slashdot.jp/story/08/12/24/0750222/IEEE%E3%82%AB%E3%83%B3%E3%83%95%E3%82%A1%E3%83%AC%E3%83%B3%E3%82%B9%E3%80%81%E8%87%AA%E5%8B%95%E7%94%9F%E6%88%90%E3%81%AE%E3%83%8B%E3%82%BB%E8%AB%96%E6%96%87%E3%82%92%E3%82%A2%E3%82%AF%E3%82%BB%E3%83%97%E3%83%88 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://heise.de/-192451 |title=Dr. Herbert Schlangemann - oder die Geschichte eines pseudowissenschaftlichen Nonsens-Papiers |last=Ziegler |first=Peter-Michael |date=December 26, 2008 |website=heise.de |publisher=Heise Zeitschriften Verlag |language=German |access-date=April 29, 2014}}</ref>

Cyril Labbé from [[Joseph Fourier University|Grenoble University]] demonstrated the vulnerability of [[h-index|''h''-index]] calculations based on [[Google Scholar]] output by feeding it a large set of SCIgen-generated documents that were citing each other, effectively an academic [[link farm]], in a 2010 paper. Using this method the author managed to rank "Ike Antkare" ahead of [[Albert Einstein]] for instance.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://rr.liglab.fr/research_report/RR-LIG-008.pdf |title=Les rapports de recherche du LIG |publisher=Rr.liglab.fr |access-date=2014-05-15}}</ref>

=== 2013 retractions ===
In 2013, over 122 published conference papers created by SCIgen were retracted by ''[[Springer Science+Business Media|Springer]]'' and the IEEE. Unlike previous submissions that were intended to be pranks, this submission were largely made by Chinese academics, who were using SCIgen papers to boost their publication record.<ref>{{Cite journal|last=Van Noorden|first=Richard|title=Publishers withdraw more than 120 gibberish papers|url=http://www.nature.com/news/publishers-withdraw-more-than-120-gibberish-papers-1.14763|journal=Nature News|year=2014|language=en|doi=10.1038/nature.2014.14763|doi-access=free}}</ref>

=== SciDetect ===
In 2015, SciDetect was released by ''Springer''. This software, developed by Cyril Labbé, is designed to automatically detect papers generated by SCIgen.<ref name=":0" />

=== 2021 report ===
In 2021, a study was published on 243 SCIgen papers that had been published in the academic literature. They found that SCIgen papers made up 75 per million papers (<{{spaces}}0.01%) in information science, and that only a small fraction of the detected papers had been dealt with.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Cabanac|first1=Guillaume|last2=Labbé|first2=Cyril|date=2021-05-25|title=Prevalence of nonsensical algorithmically generated papers in the scientific literature|journal=Journal of the Association for Information Science and Technology|volume=72|issue=12|language=en|pages=1461–1476|doi=10.1002/asi.24495|s2cid=236374033|issn=2330-1635|doi-access=free}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|last=Noorden|first=Richard Van|date=2021-05-27|title=Hundreds of gibberish papers still lurk in the scientific literature|url=https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-021-01436-7|journal=Nature|language=en|volume=594|issue=7862|pages=160–161|doi=10.1038/d41586-021-01436-7|pmid=34045760|bibcode=2021Natur.594..160V|s2cid=235232305|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

